WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Observe demand. The application serves visitors while metrics record utilization and request behavior. Select a signal related to the constrained resource, not simply the easiest chart.

2
00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Choose a target. The controller compares the observed signal with a configured target and computes a desired replica count within minimum and maximum limits.

3
00:01:18.000 --> 00:02:00.917
Request capacity. The controller requests more replicas. Scheduling, image download, initialization and health checks take time. Desired capacity and ready capacity can differ.

4
00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:42.167
Wait for readiness. Only ready replicas should receive normal traffic. If nodes or quotas are exhausted, a controller cannot create the missing physical capacity by itself.

5
00:02:42.167 --> 00:03:16.250
Check dependencies. More application copies can increase database connections. Limit aggregate concurrency so solving an application bottleneck does not create a database outage.

6
00:03:16.250 --> 00:04:03.417
Scale down carefully. After demand falls, stabilization prevents rapid shrinking and regrowth. Drain work before removing replicas and keep enough minimum capacity for the next burst.
